elastic fiber morphology, abnormal isoelectric focusing of serum transferrin, abnormal skull morphology\n\nThoracic Mass/Neoplasm vs Central Nervous System Lesion (e.g., Stroke):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the scrotum, abnormality of tibia morphology, aplasia/hypoplasia of the breasts, hemivertebrae, hypermelanotic macule\n  Favours second: abnormal csf protein level, abnormal visual field test, amaurosis fugax, aphasia, ataxia\n\nThoracic Mass/Neoplasm vs Vascular Occlusion:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the scrotum, abnormality of tibia morphology, aplasia/hypoplasia of the breasts, hemivertebrae, hypermelanotic macule\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nCentral Nervous System Lesion (e.g., Stroke) vs Vascular Occlusion:\n  Favours first:  abnormal csf protein level, abnormal visual field test, amaurosis fugax, aphasia, bladder or bowel problems, including difficulty (or hesitancy) when trying to pee or a feeling of urgency\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nRelated to Peripheral Neuropathy: acquired peripheral neuropathy, autoimmune neuropathy, autonomic neuropathy\n\nRelated to Thoracic Mass/Neoplasm: benign axillary neoplasm, benign neoplasm, benign neoplasm of chest wall\n\nRelated to Central Nervous System Lesion (e.g., Stroke): behcet disease, benign angiitis of the central nervous system, central nervous system aids arteritis\n\nRelated to Vascular Occlusion: arterial occlusion, retinal artery occlusion, vascular disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Peripheral Neuropathy\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → upper limb asymmetry → Thoracic Mass/Neoplasm"}
